e crucial thing: that student who might have been placed with you almost certainly grew up in the shadow of the very extremism you're pointing to. They're not a product of it in the sense of being indoctrinated; they're a survivor of it. If your documentary spent any time on the victims, it would have shown you people like them. The fact that it apparently didn't only reinforces how skewed the picture you've been given really is. You can absolutely hold onto the belief that what you watched confirms your fear. But I'd ask you to consider: did that documentary provide any evidence whatsoever that an Afghan student accepted into a carefully vetted international hosting programme poses a danger? Or did it just stoke a generalised anxiety that you're now applying indiscriminately? Because those are two very different things. You're not going to find the answer in a TV special. You'd find it by meeting an actual person and seeing whether the stereotype holds. But you don't have to do that if you're not willing. I'm just asking you to recognise that a cable documentary designed to terrify you about a region is not a fair or complete basis for judging an individual human being who had the courage to leave.
